WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.890
- You know him from Magic
Mike, 21 Jump Street,

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.110
and possibly that sex
dream you had last night.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.360
That's right, that's right.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.130
Give it up for Channing Tatum, everybody.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:08.963
(soft melodic music)

00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:12.472
(audience yells and claps)

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:13.740
- Hello!

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.930
- Hi. How are you?

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.470
- I am so good but you look like somewhere

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.730
wonderful behind you.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.730
I want to be where you're at.
- Ah, yeah.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:21.644
- [Kelly] Yeah, that looks awesome.

00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:22.477
(Channing laughs)

00:00:22.477 --> 00:00:23.310
- Come on over. We could-

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.380
- I'm like, it looks like
I want to take a walk

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.040
in your garden or your park behind you.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.010
I don't know what's happening,
but why did you think...

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.080
'cause you're such a fitness guy.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.770
What did you think of Meg's Fitness Story?

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.080
It's pretty incredible, right?

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.830
- Unreal. I mean, look, as
someone that has to work out

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.640
as for a job, I promise you
I would not look like this

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.760
unless I had to be naked in
most of my movies mostly.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.620
And like, at some point I
got to get better at acting,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.358
so I don't have to be
naked in all of them,

00:00:47.358 --> 00:00:48.191
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:48.191 --> 00:00:50.023
but she is just incredible.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.920
I mean, I literally get
to work out as a job

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.200
and it's still hard.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.250
And I can't imagine people
that have a nine to five job,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.830
have kids at home, and then
where do they get the energy

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.480
and the time to actually, you
know, focus on themselves.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.850
And I just think it's really
beautiful what she's done.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.720
- Absolutely. And you get it, you're dad,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.790
it's like there's a lot of time for work,

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:15.680
and then you're a dad,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.333
It's like, it's a lot,
like to fit everything in.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.630
Do you do like massive cheat days?

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.890
'Cause Dwayne Johnson was on here

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.780
and he showed me his
cheat day and I was like,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.260
first of all, I would barf if I ate all

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.580
the things he ate on his cheat day.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.653
But do you have massive ones?

00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:36.570
- I sometimes do, not like massive ones.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:37.670
'Cause I think as I'm getting older,

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.760
it's really getting, like
everything I pick up,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.970
I just go, Oh, that's like two more miles

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.240
that I have to run or ride,
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.010
and it's like, I just
started looking at things

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.040
like they're just actual miles,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.960
and time that I have to work out.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.530
So I will pick the day and I
will pick very specific things.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.305
I love cake.
- Oh my God, me too.

00:01:55.305 --> 00:01:57.217
- But yeah, it's-
(Channing laughs)

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.050
- Oh my God.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:58.930
And you just had a birthday.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.170
We were birthdays near each other.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.620
I'm the 24th and you're
the 26th right, of April?

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:04.453
- Of course, man.

00:02:04.453 --> 00:02:06.620
We are absolutely-
- We are running the world.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.570
- Yeah, I just had it, just had it.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.410
- I don't know one Taurus
that doesn't love cake.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.250
I'm just saying Lester's
birthday is today,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.083
and he loves cake.

00:02:13.083 --> 00:02:14.192
I'm just saying, but he also-

00:02:14.192 --> 00:02:15.720
- Is that a cake thing?
Is that a Taurus thing?

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.190
- I think so I'm owning it.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.530
I'm claiming it, it's not my fault.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.603
I was born into this.

00:02:19.603 --> 00:02:20.510
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.670
I was born this way, Gaga.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.060
So how did you celebrate?

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.689
- We really did, like we chilled.

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:29.400
I think even during
quarantine like last year,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.220
it was just a really
chill sort of birthday.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.530
And we just sat around the pool,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.010
we sidewalk chucked my driveway,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.560
we have a giant wolf
that looks like it's...

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.200
we traced all the light
shadows from the trees

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.590
and it looks like this wolf on fire.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.980
It's like a Wolf Walker on fire wolf,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.849
that has like chicken
legs for some reason.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.140
I don't know why.
- Oh my God, I love it.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.660
- I think neither one of my daughter

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.350
or I knew how to draw wolf feet,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.710
so we just went with chicken.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.550
- Oh my gosh. Have you
seen the thing on Netflix?

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.451
I think it's called like
Wolf Walker or something.

00:02:57.451 --> 00:02:58.790
My kids were obsessed with it.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:02:59.623
I can't remember the name of it

00:02:59.623 --> 00:03:01.190
but it won all these awards.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.250
It was so good but then-

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.120
- It's so good that that's our
favorite movie at the moment.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.411
Same filmmaker as Song of the Sea,

00:03:08.411 --> 00:03:10.600
which you should definitely see the music,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.200
and it is just outstanding.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.760
- Oh, wow! I started watching
one last night with my kids

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.270
and I was like, "Is this the same people

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.900
that made the wolf one?"

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.850
And anyway, I've seen it so many times,

00:03:20.805 --> 00:03:21.638
- Yeah.

00:03:21.638 --> 00:03:23.260
- Is it true though that
your first big challenge

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.180
with fatherhood was
learning how to braid hair?

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.520
- Yeah. I completely
panicked when I found out

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.690
we were having a girl and I
literally went to YouTube,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.848
and started figuring out like how...

00:03:33.848 --> 00:03:36.060
I think the first one I
saw was a dad vacuuming up

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.390
his daughter's hair with like
a hair tie on to the vacuum.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.780
And I was like, that's genius.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.200
And then it doesn't work
like the guy did it.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.430
I tried it and it didn't work as well.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.504
But yeah, so I learned how to braid hair,

00:03:47.504 --> 00:03:49.180
the hardest thing though,

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.160
'cause my daughter has
really straight, fine hair,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:52.350
I have to do this every night

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:53.923
or she wakes up with a rat's nest.

00:03:53.923 --> 00:03:57.584
It's like a giant, not back there.

00:03:57.584 --> 00:04:00.080
But the hardest thing
'cause she has layered hair,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.732
is doing the three, close
to the head, equal strands.

00:04:03.732 --> 00:04:06.420
- [Kelly] Yup.
- You know, once I got that,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.410
the braiding's easy.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.610
The braiding's really, really simple,

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:11.630
but I'll get like three or
a third of the way through,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.460
and I'll realize that one strand

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.950
is a lot shorter than the other one,

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.406
and my OCD kicks in and
I have to like restart,

00:04:17.406 --> 00:04:19.330
and then it's just terrible.

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.130
- I know, God bless
sports for me on that one.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.850
We all used to braid each
other in volleyball and soccer,

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.809
all those sports, I learned
that at a very young age.

00:04:24.809 --> 00:04:25.642
(audience claps)

00:04:25.642 --> 00:04:26.873
- His new children's
book is called, "The One

00:04:26.873 --> 00:04:29.693
and Only Sparkella,"
and it's out right now.

00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:31.920
But have you always wanted
to write a kid's book?

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.730
'Cause I didn't think one.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:35.330
I'd ever have a kid and then I had kids,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.380
and then I was like, oh,
it'd be fun to write a book,

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.020
you know, you're reading them
all the time with your kids,

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:39.900
and I ended up doing it too.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.084
Is that kind of how it happened for you?

00:04:42.084 --> 00:04:43.820
- Sort of. I mean, I never thought

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.670
I'd be an author of anything.
- Me neither.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.060
- Much less like a kids book.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.922
And, but like what
you're saying, you know,

00:04:48.922 --> 00:04:51.604
you definitely end up
reading almost all of them.

00:04:51.604 --> 00:04:53.440
You were looking for any suggestions

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.074
'cause we read five books every night.

00:04:55.074 --> 00:04:57.790
All my friends were like, "You read five?

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.330
That was a horrible idea.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.600
Why didn't you just say it's one book?"

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.364
And I was like, it's too late, we started,

00:05:02.364 --> 00:05:03.950
we started with five already,

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.950
it's not the guys cast.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.240
So we were reading
everything under the sun,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.590
and then I definitely realized that

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.970
there weren't that many
dad-daughter books.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.380
There was a kind of a hole in the market.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.680
There was a lot of mom-kid
books and certain other ones,

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.400
and I just sort of was like,
you know, I don't know.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.904
I wanted to write something that was very,

00:05:24.904 --> 00:05:27.615
just me and my daughter have a
really beautiful relationship

00:05:27.615 --> 00:05:29.650
and it was really fun.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.944
And, you know, I was worried,
I was worried a little bit.

00:05:31.944 --> 00:05:34.884
It was just how being a dad
of how was I going to relate

00:05:34.884 --> 00:05:37.844
and understand a little girl.

00:05:37.844 --> 00:05:42.560
And the key was just to go into
her world and it was so fun,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.260
and the world that we created was so rich,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.040
and like the characters, and the voices,

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.610
and the things was so much fun.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.964
And she's such a little
character that I don't know.

00:05:51.964 --> 00:05:54.796
The story just sort of happened.

00:05:54.796 --> 00:05:58.050
I didn't really have to like
dig deep for the imagination.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.476
It was more-
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:59.476 --> 00:06:00.430
- it was more just observing my daughter,

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.554
and really just our relationship
is super hilarious to me.

00:06:03.554 --> 00:06:06.810
- Yeah. If you're anything
like the father in that book,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.300
you're awesome and you're nailing it.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.560
It was the tutu and how you were coming,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.455
and my kids loved it.

00:06:12.455 --> 00:06:14.510
They were like, I wonder
if daddy would dress up

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.220
and not their dad probably, totally would,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.950
but it was just it totally
got their minds thinking,

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.390
like you said there's not a lot of dad,

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:21.223
I really can't...

00:06:21.223 --> 00:06:22.980
I thought I was thinking that Nelly knew

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.680
that from the Llama Llama series,

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:25.720
there's only one book-
- Yeah.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:26.570
- that's kind of what the dad,

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:27.780
and they paint this whole thing.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.900
But other than that, I was like,
I don't really know of any.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.520
So it's a really good idea.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.270
And it's so important
'cause father figures

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.800
are so important for kids.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.900
And I love Sparkella and I
loved that she tried to fit in

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.140
and then she comes back.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.200
Like my kids, I mean,
they're hard on books.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.080
We read every night,
just like you apparently.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.560
And they're hard on books
and they will just walk away.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.193
They'll be like, I'm done,
I just want to go to bed.

00:06:48.193 --> 00:06:49.026
(Channing laughs)

00:06:49.026 --> 00:06:50.590
Like they can be hard on authors.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.000
I'm like, you're a
critic, but they loved it.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.170
And they were captivated the whole time.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.974
And you did a really good job with it.

00:06:56.974 --> 00:06:58.473
- Thank you. That means-

00:06:58.473 --> 00:07:01.890
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- That means a lot to me.

00:07:02.917 --> 00:07:04.460
In the same way she
really is hard on books.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.560
She's hard even on me when she was little,

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.480
I was trying so hard to be entertaining,

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.430
and doing all the voices and everything.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.811
And she'd be like, "Dad, can
you just read it straight?"

00:07:15.811 --> 00:07:18.750
- Oh my gosh. Same.
- I just want it straight.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.040
And I'm like, "I've been
training for this my whole life.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.040
And you don't want me to do the voices?"

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.001
- Yeah, you didn't like my English accent?

00:07:25.001 --> 00:07:26.451
(everybody laughs)

00:07:26.451 --> 00:07:28.000
- I was like, look, I'm
never gonna be doing

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.120
those accent in the movie.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.203
Let me do it with you.
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:31.203 --> 00:07:33.253
(Channing laughs)

00:07:33.253 --> 00:07:35.203
- I love it.
- But yeah, I don't know.

00:07:38.356 --> 00:07:42.550
She's a hard critic, and
she, like the glitter poop

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:43.750
and stuff like that.
- Glitter poop my kids love.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.190
And we, we really do that and
she found it really funny.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.210
- My kids were cracking up.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:49.040
My little boy was cracking
up at glitter poop.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.319
He's like, "you said, poop."

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:51.483
He's five.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.210
But anyways, is your
daughter anything like,

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.720
are you like the characters?

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.290
She's all that kind of sparkle,

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.960
rock and roll, little girl walking in?

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.850
- Yeah. You know, there
was a really kind of,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.690
where the whole Genesis of the story,

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.680
she was in preschool
and she had this like,

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.210
she had this watermelon
cape sort of thing,

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.540
that when you held your arms out,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.220
it looked like she was
a slice of watermelon

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:16.280
but like when they're down,

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.290
she just looked like a cool little cape.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:19.570
And I remember she loved it,

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:20.530
I don't even know where we got it.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.250
I don't remember buying it.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.670
And she loved it and I
remember the day she put it on

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:25.503
to go to school,

00:08:25.503 --> 00:08:27.050
and then when I was like,
"Alright, alright babe,

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.390
it's time to go. Let's go."

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:31.090
She like panicked and she
started taking it off,

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:31.923
she's like, "I don't want to wear it,

00:08:31.923 --> 00:08:32.949
I don't wanna wear it."

00:08:32.949 --> 00:08:35.359
And then I was like, "Wait,
what's what's wrong?"

00:08:35.359 --> 00:08:37.859
And she's like, "I don't
want to get made fun of."

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.900
And I realized, that
was the very first time

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.810
the least that I saw of her
being like self-conscious

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.280
about how other people saw her,

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.790
and it was like
heartbreaking, but you know,

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.750
a beautiful moment all in
one, but she really did.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.790
That was like a small phase
and she really did come out

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.450
of any kind of, I think
self consciousness in a way.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.050
She's a little precautious
little tricks too.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:03.883
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:03.883 --> 00:09:04.840
- She's very much like Sparkella,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:09.300
and I don't know, maybe I
like to wear a fuchsia bow

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:12.380
a little too easily,
maybe I like my nails...

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.520
I'm not sure okay.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.856
- But if you pull it
off, you'll put it off.

00:09:15.856 --> 00:09:18.590
(both laughs)

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.216
- It's fun. Anything that make her laugh,

00:09:21.216 --> 00:09:23.490
it's a good idea.
- I love it, I love it.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:24.900
It's really is a good thing for dads,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.210
it's a really cool thing you're doing.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.890
I have a question, did you...

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.330
my mom is an educator.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.250
So like we read a lot when I was a kid.

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:32.980
Did your parents do the same for you?

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.890
Is that why you read a lot with her?

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.580
- Yeah, my mom read a lot.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.580
My dad worked a lot.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.200
My mom worked too but I think I remember

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.250
my mom reading most with me

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:44.830
and they kept all my books.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:48.440
They were all like, you
know, the golden spine.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.212
- I collect them-

00:09:50.212 --> 00:09:53.420
- Yeah, yeah. My daughter
got to read them.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.020
So it was pretty cool,

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.600
and it was such a bizarre sort of trip

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.750
through memory lane to like open up a book

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.180
that you probably didn't see or read.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.220
And like last time, you were
probably eight or seven.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.360
- Yeah.
- And to get to now do it

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.728
at 40 was like a really strange like trip.

00:10:08.728 --> 00:10:10.690
But yeah, I think they did,

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.230
they read a good book to me.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.420
- Yeah. Well, it is lovely
to see you, Channing.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.890
Good luck on the book, it's amazing!

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.060
- I'm such a fan of you.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.021
I just want you to know,

00:10:20.021 --> 00:10:21.500
I'm so happy that you're doing this-

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.280
- Oh, thank you!
- And people are getting

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.113
to see you.

00:10:24.113 --> 00:10:26.940
I'm like a real fan.
You're such a big heart.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.700
